# .gitattributes
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# This file tells Git how to treat the line endings and file types in THIS repo.
# It travels with the repository, so every machine you clone to (all your PCs)
# follows the same rules automatically. This is what stops the "every file shows
# as modified" problem when you switch computers.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# For every file, let Git auto-detect whether it's text, and if it is text,
# store it in the repository using LF ("\n") line endings AND check it out with
# LF on disk too. "eol=lf" forces LF everywhere, on Windows included, which keeps
# things consistent with your Linux/Azure deployment target.
* text=auto eol=lf

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary files: mark these as binary so Git NEVER tries to "fix" their line
# endings. (That's what was silently rewriting your resume PDF.) Binary files
# are compared and stored byte-for-byte, untouched.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.pdf   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.eot   binary
*.zip   binary

# This owner-approved visual-authority package is retained byte-for-byte so its
# supplied SHA-256 manifests remain reproducible. Some source prototypes use
# intentional Markdown hard breaks or trailing indentation.
docs/initiatives/PS-INTERVIEW-FOCUS-UI-001/source-authority/** whitespace=-trailing-space
